  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThe potential for wet weather is coming into better focus this \nweekend. A cool continental airmass in the lower levels should \ncontinue spreading across Texas on Saturday. A shortwave is forecast \nto amplify and sag southward as a nearly cut-off low as it \napproaches Texas, redirecting moisture over our area as mid-level \nwinds shift southwesterly. This will also result in the development \nof a lee-side trough on the eastern flanks of the Mexican plateau, \nwhich in turn increases the supply of low-level moisture from the \nGulf. The combination of these two moisture streams ascending atop \ncool air supports a broad rain event beginning Saturday morning or \nafternoon along the Rio Grande and Edwards Plateau, then spreading \nacross much of South-Central Texas Saturday night through Sunday \nbefore rain chances lessen Monday with the lee trough dropping \nsoutheast and transitioning into a coastal low. \n\nThe highest rain chances and rainfall totals are likely to be where \nthe low-level and mid-level moisture streams overlap the most. This \nincludes areas along the Rio Grande eastward towards the San Antonio \narea, where rain chances rise Saturday, especially in the evening, \nand then climb to about 90 percent on Sunday. Peak rainfall chances \ntail off only slightly to about 50-70 percent elsewhere, though \nmodels do suggest that amounts are likely to decrease towards the \nnortheast farther away from the low level supply of moisture. Models \nhave trended upwards in the amount of rainfall from this event, with \nthe GEFS and ENS starting to show a broader area of 1 to 2 inch \nrainfall totals in their ensemble means. Some of the deterministic \nand ML-based guidance is also beginning to depict a few swaths of 2 \nto 4 inches. The isentropic and cool nature of the rains will be a \nlimiting factor for rain rates, so this event looks more like a \nsteady soaking and beneficial rain. The rain and clouds will keep \nconditions over the weekend cool with temperatures struggling to get \nabove the 60s Sunday. If the damp weather overperforms, a few spots \nmay struggle to warm above the 50s outright. \n\nConditions should dry and gradually warm once the moisture is \ncarried farther east. Rain chances linger over the Coastal Plains \nMonday in closer proximity to the coastal low, but by Tuesday models \nare in better agreement that the trough responsible for marshaling \nthe weekend's moisture will be out of the area with drier air \nfollowing behind. Afternoon temperatures are forecast to return to \nthe 70s by midweek next week with overnight lows mostly in the 50s.",
